===== TURN 1 — USER =====
For my Master's thesis I want to do something about ML monitoring. At my student job our churn model quietly got worse for months before anyone noticed — is there a thesis in that?

===== TURN 1 — AGENT =====
There's likely a thesis in this, but the interesting part isn't "the model got worse" — it's *why nobody noticed*. That's actually two different failure modes wearing the same costume.

Before I go dig up literature: when your churn model degraded, was it that the *predictions* silently drifted (score distributions shifting, calibration going off) — or was it that nobody could even *check* accuracy in real time because you only find out if someone actually churned weeks or months later? Which one was it, or was it both tangled together?

===== TURN 2 — USER =====
Honestly both, but mostly the second — churn labels only arrive weeks later. We did have a dashboard with drift scores, but it alerted so often that everyone ignored it, and nobody could say whether a high score actually meant the model was worse.

===== TURN 2 — AGENT =====
That helps place it. The closest existing work splits into two camps: **Xu & Klabjan (2021)** build a drift-detection ensemble specifically for the *lagged-labels* setting — your exact churn situation — and **Tan et al. (2025)** attack the *alert-fatigue* side directly, using statistical process control to control false-positive rate in label-less drift detection. There's also work (Woo et al. 2025) estimating accuracy without any labels at all via model agreement.

So the "detect drift without labels" problem and the "stop crying wolf" problem are both already being worked on separately. What none of these seem to do is close the loop back to what actually happened weeks later — i.e., check whether their drift alerts *would have* predicted the real accuracy drop once the labels finally arrived.

Here's the question that matters: when your dashboard's drift score was high but you couldn't tell if the model was actually worse — do you know, in hindsight, whether it *was* worse at those moments? Do you (or could you) still get your hands on that history — drift scores, predictions, and the churn labels that eventually landed — or would this have to run on public/synthetic data instead?

===== TURN 3 — USER =====
In hindsight it really was worse at some of those moments, but I can't use company data for the thesis — it would have to be public datasets with delayed labels simulated. I like that closing-the-loop angle: do the drift scores actually predict the accuracy drop once labels arrive. Can you save where we are so far? This is for my Master's.

